/-- A bundled positive-stepsize composite prox selector supplies an existing prox point.

For any abstract candidate type, if each current point, oracle vector, and
positive stepsize has a selected minimizer of the composite prox objective, then
the corresponding `IsCompositeProxPoint` existence statement follows.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (composite prox selector existence bridge)
Proof: choose the point component supplied by the selector at `(x, g, γ)` and
  use its stored `IsMinOn` certificate after unfolding the composite objective
  predicate.
Source: Mathlib subtype projections and order argmin predicates for real-valued
  objective minimization
Used in: nonconvex stochastic mirror descent prox-step existence from the
  paper argmin update selector
Book citation: book/FOML/NonconvexStochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, two-phase randomized stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem compositeProxPoint_exists_of_selector
    {E P : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    (V : P → P → ℝ) (h : P → ℝ) (eval : P → E)
    (proxMap :
      ∀ p : P × E × {γ : ℝ // 0 < γ},
        {z : P //
          IsMinOn
            (fun u : P => ⟪p.2.1, eval u⟫_ℝ + p.2.2.1⁻¹ * V p.1 u + h u)
            Set.univ z})
    (x : P) (g : E) {γ : ℝ} (hγ : 0 < γ) :
    ∃ xplus : P,
      IsMinOn
        (fun u : P => ⟪g, eval u⟫_ℝ + γ⁻¹ * V x u + h u)
        Set.univ xplus := by
  let p : P × E × {γ : ℝ // 0 < γ} := (x, g, ⟨γ, hγ⟩)
  refine ⟨(proxMap p).1, ?_⟩
  simpa [p] using (proxMap p).2

/-- A selected positive-stepsize composite prox point satisfies the paper prox predicate
whenever the local and paper argmin predicates are pointwise equivalent.

This abstracts the common bridge where an algorithm-local prox predicate is
proved for a selected update, while the paper-facing statement uses a
definitionally equal argmin predicate with different notation.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (selected prox predicate transport)
Proof: specialize the local selected-point certificate and transport it across
  the pointwise iff between local and paper argmin predicates.
Source: Mathlib propositional equivalence and dependent function specialization APIs
Used in: nonconvex stochastic mirror descent prox-step selected argmin
  certificate normalized to the paper prox predicate
Book citation: book/FOML/NonconvexStochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, two-phase randomized stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem compositeProxPoint_isPaperProxPoint
    {E P : Type*}
    (selector : P → E → ∀ γ : ℝ, 0 < γ → P)
    (localPred paperPred : P → E → ℝ → P → Prop)
    (hlocal : ∀ (x : P) (g : E) (γ : ℝ) (hγ : 0 < γ),
      localPred x g γ (selector x g γ hγ))
    (hiff : ∀ (x : P) (g : E) (γ : ℝ) (z : P),
      localPred x g γ z ↔ paperPred x g γ z)
    (x : P) (g : E) (γ : ℝ) (hγ : 0 < γ) :
    paperPred x g γ (selector x g γ hγ) := by
  exact (hiff x g γ (selector x g γ hγ)).1 (hlocal x g γ hγ)

/-- A selected composite prox point satisfies the composite prox argmin predicate.

For an abstract candidate type, a bundled positive-stepsize prox selector
returns a point together with its `IsMinOn` certificate; the point projection is
therefore a composite prox point for the corresponding objective.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (composite prox selector specification bridge)
Proof: unfold the selected prox point, composite prox predicate, and composite
  prox objective, then project the stored `IsMinOn` certificate from the
  bundled selector.
Source: Mathlib subtype projections and order argmin predicates for real-valued
  objective minimization
Used in: nonconvex stochastic mirror descent prox-step selected argmin
  certificate from the paper update selector
Book citation: book/FOML/NonconvexStochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, two-phase randomized stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem compositeProxPoint_isProxPoint
    {E P : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    (V : P → P → ℝ) (h : P → ℝ) (eval : P → E)
    (proxMap :
      ∀ p : P × E × {γ : ℝ // 0 < γ},
        {z : P //
          IsMinOn
            (fun u : P => ⟪p.2.1, eval u⟫_ℝ + p.2.2.1⁻¹ * V p.1 u + h u)
            Set.univ z})
    (x : P) (g : E) (γ : ℝ) (hγ : 0 < γ) :
    IsMinOn
      (fun u : P => ⟪g, eval u⟫_ℝ + γ⁻¹ * V x u + h u)
      Set.univ ((proxMap (x, g, ⟨γ, hγ⟩)).1) := by
  let p : P × E × {γ : ℝ // 0 < γ} := (x, g, ⟨γ, hγ⟩)
  simpa [p] using (proxMap p).2

/-- Exact projected-gradient mapping evaluated at a run-selected output point.

This is the abstract model wrapper for quantities of the form `g_X(\bar x_s)`:
first choose the run output at `s` and sample point `ω`, then evaluate the exact
